[38;5;231m⏺[39m Current state: a minimal CommonJS Node project — [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m prints a greeting, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m has [38;5;153mgreet()[39m. No React,
  no bundler, no test setup, no dependencies at all. So a React todo list means standing up a frontend stack from
  scratch here, which is why this is architectural rather than a bounded edit.

  First question, and it's the one that shapes everything downstream.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether todos survive a page reload. That decides if this is purely a UI exercise or if it needs a storage
  layer and, potentially, a backend.

  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — state lives in React, gone on refresh. Cheapest, fastest to build, fine for a demo or a React
    learning exercise. Cheap to upgrade later.
  - [1mlocalStorage[0m — survives reload, still zero backend. Adds a small persistence module and the load/save edge cases
    (corrupt data, first run). Also cheap to upgrade later.
  - [1mReal backend + API[0m — survives across devices, needs a server, an API contract, and probably a database.
    Substantially more work, and it's the one choice that's expensive to unwind since it shapes the data flow
    throughout.

  [1mMy recommendation: localStorage.[0m It's a genuinely useful todo list rather than a toy, it keeps the whole thing
  frontend-only, and the persistence boundary stays a single small module you could later swap for an API client without
  touching the components.
